/* GENERATED by tools/build-guide.js from FOR-TRAPPERS.md — do not edit by hand. */
window.OPOSSUM_FOOT_GUIDE_HTML = "<p>A trap log built by a trapper, for trappers. If you can run a trap line, you can run this app. This guide walks through everything one tap at a time.</p>\n<h3>The idea in thirty seconds</h3>\n<p>You already know where your sets are. What you don't know — not really — is what's <em>producing</em>. Which lure, which bait, which trap, which location is actually putting fur up? Write it down every time in this app, and at the end of the season it tells you. No more guessing in February.</p>\n<h3>Putting it on your phone</h3>\n<p><strong>iPhone:</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Open the beta link in Safari (not Messenger, not Facebook — Safari).</li><li>Tap the Share button (the square with the arrow pointing up).</li><li>Scroll down and tap \"Add to Home Screen.\"</li><li>Tap \"Add.\" You'll see the Opossum Foot icon on your home screen now. Open</li></ol>\n<p>the app from that icon from here on out.</p>\n<p><strong>Android:</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Open the beta link in Chrome.</li><li>Tap the three-dot menu in the corner.</li><li>Tap \"Add to Home screen\" (or \"Install app\").</li><li>Open the app from the new icon.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>If a link comes to you in Messenger:</strong> Messenger can't add things to your home screen. Long-press the link, tap Copy, open Safari (or Chrome) yourself, paste it in the address bar, and follow the steps above.</p>\n<h3>The first time you open it</h3>\n<p>The app asks you to pick your state. Pick the state you trap in — the map zooms right to it, and your season reminders come from that state's rules. You can change it later in Settings.</p>\n<p>Then it asks where your home ground is. Pick \"Place it on the map\" — pan to your spot, tap to drop the pin, and drag it onto the exact place — or \"Use my GPS location\" to set it where you're standing. Every time you open the app, the map starts there. Each trap line gets its own home ground — your Iowa line and your buddy's-farm line don't have to start in the same place. You can change it any time in Settings.</p>\n<h3>The tabs along the bottom</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Map</strong> — Your sets as pins on the map. Green: active. Amber: sprung.</li></ul>\n<p>Gray: pulled. Blue: missing. Red means one thing: the set is past its trap-check interval (see below). Tap any pin to see that set.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Scorecard</strong> — Which lure, bait, and urine is actually putting fur up,</li></ul>\n<p>ranked by raw catch counts. Filter by season or species, or put two lures head-to-head.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Lines</strong> — Your trap lines, each with its own mileage log.</li><li><strong>History</strong> — Every catch and event you've logged. Flip between By date</li></ul>\n<p>and By Trap Type, search the lot, filter by disposition, and switch between this season, last season, and all time. The <strong>Activity</strong> view is the sets' diary: every edit, status change, pull, and other event, dated, with the weather — the same trail sits on each set's own page, under <strong>Activity</strong>. (Species counts live on Totals now.) (Reports and CSV exports live on the Data tab.)</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Totals</strong> — Your season numbers in four cards: By Species, By Set, By</li></ul>\n<p>Disposition, and By Month. Rows show the title and kept count only — tap one to see the details. Tap a card's header to fold it away or open it back up.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Seasons</strong> — Season dates and reminders for your state, species by</li></ul>\n<p>species.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Paperwork</strong> — Your license photos plus the landowner notebook: names,</li></ul>\n<p>phone numbers, permission slips, and boundary notes for every property you run. The <strong>Photo gallery</strong> button sits beside the License Wallet and gathers every photo on the line in one place.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Settings</strong> — the Toggles menus (features, tabs on the bottom bar,</li></ul>\n<p>fields on the set form), Backup &amp; Reports, and Erase Everything.</p>\n<h3>Trap lines</h3>\n<p>A \"line\" is a group of sets you run together — say, your home line and the line on your buddy's farm. Each line keeps its own sets, logs, seasons, licenses, history, and totals, all separate.</p>\n<ul><li>Only one line is active at a time. The brass-colored bar near the top of</li></ul>\n<p>the screen always shows which line you're on — tap it to switch.</p>\n<ul><li>To start a new line: go to the Lines tab and tap \"+ Add a trap line.\" Give</li></ul>\n<p>it a name and pick its state.</p>\n<ul><li>When you start out, everything lives on Line 1. That's fine.</li></ul>\n<h3>Adding a set — step by step</h3>\n<ol><li>Go to the <strong>Map</strong> tab.</li><li>Tap the <strong>+</strong> button (or the middle pin button to place the pin somewhere</li></ol>\n<p>you're not standing: tap the map, drag the pin onto the exact spot, and hit \"Looks right\"). A form opens. While you're placing a pin, the rest of the app waits — finish the placement first.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Name your set.</strong> This one's required — the form names it for you</li></ol>\n<p>(\"Trap 1,\" then \"Trap 2,\" and if you rename one \"Creek crossing\" the next becomes \"Creek crossing 2\"). Change it any time.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Trap:</strong> picking starts with the manufacturer — Bridger, Duke, Minnesota</li></ol>\n<p>Brand, on down the list — then the type, then the size, so you land on the exact trap in three taps. Don't know the brand? Pick \"Not sure / mixed brands\" and you get the full unfiltered lists like before. Don't see your trap, spring, or size in the list at all? Every dropdown has an \"Other…\" at the bottom — pick it, type what's actually in your hand, and the app remembers it on that line next time. Below that there's an optional Mods section — pan size, lamination, whatever you've done to the trap since it left the factory. It defaults to stock, so skip it if you run them as-bought.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Snare purpose</strong> (snares only) — Kill snare or Live-hold snare. Pick</li></ol>\n<p>the honest one; it rides the set record and the exports.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Bait, lure, urine, and other attractants</strong> — what the set is carrying.</li></ol>\n<p>Each gets its own stack of rows: bait, lure, urine, plus Other Attractants for the rest of it — feathers, squeakers, T-bones, foil, whatever you're running. Tap \"+ Add Another\" to stack more (up to six per field). The boxes work by typing: start typing and the matches appear — the list stays shut until you type. Everything you've run before is remembered, so a letter or two brings it right back, and there's a \"None\" in every box for a set that carries nothing there. Fill these in honestly — this is what makes the Totals and Scorecard tabs worth anything.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Set type</strong> — Flat, Pocket, Dirt hole, and the rest, grouped Land and</li></ol>\n<p>Water, in one A–Z run. A type you type in yourself under \"Other…\" is remembered and joins the A–Z list for good; \"Other…\" itself always stays at the bottom.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Status</strong> — five buttons now, not a dropdown: Active, Sprung, Pulled,</li></ol>\n<p>Missing, and Prospect (a spot you're planning on but haven't set yet). New sets start Active. If a trap goes missing, the app asks why: Stolen, Mother Nature, Can't locate, Removed, or Confiscated (that one asks for the details and lets you snap the citation). Find it again and \"Mark recovered\" puts it back to Pulled.</p>\n<ol><li>Snap the set right when you make it — the form has a photo button now,</li></ol>\n<p>so you can record exactly how you built it before you walk away.</p>\n<ol><li>Tap <strong>Save set.</strong> Your pin shows up on the map.</li></ol>\n<p>Running a line of identical sets? The form carries your whole rig forward from the last set — same trap, same mods, same set type, same bait and lure rows — so it's mostly tapping Save. Name, status, date, location, photos, and notes never carry over; those are always fresh.</p>\n<p>Changed something at a set — fresh bait, pulled the trap? Open the set from the map pin and edit it. The app keeps the history straight — every edit, status change, and pull files in the set's Activity trail, on its page and in History.</p>\n<p>Red asterisks on the set form mean the same as on the log form — the app won't save without them. The coordinates sit at the bottom of the form (and the set detail too) — tap them to copy, handy when you're texting a buddy your spot.</p>\n<p><strong>Notes on a set are dated entries.</strong> Type in the Notes box and tap <strong>＋ Add note</strong> — the note files with today's date, newest first, so the set's story reads in order (\"rebaited with Ghost Dancer,\" \"sign everywhere but no takers yet\"). Notes you wrote before entries existed carry in as one undated \"Earlier note.\" Dictated memos file as dated entries too, the moment you stop recording.</p>\n<h3>Logging a catch — step by step</h3>\n<ol><li>Tap the set's pin on the map, then tap</li></ol>\n<p><strong>\"＋ Log a catch.\"</strong> (History shows you what happened — the pin is where you log what's happening.)</p>\n<ol><li>The sheet opens with <strong>Catch</strong> and <strong>Dispatched</strong> already picked,</li></ol>\n<p>because nine times out of ten that's what happened. The red asterisk marks what's required.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Pick the animal.</strong> The list runs A to Z with a search box at the top —</li></ol>\n<p>type \"coon\" and Raccoon pops right up. If it was a neighbor's dog or a house cat, that's the \"Domestic animal\" row at the very bottom (tap the <strong>?</strong> next to it for what to do).</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Count</strong> — how many.</li><li><strong>Disposition</strong> — what happened to it:</li></ol>\n<p>- <strong>Dispatched</strong> — it's going on the stretcher. - <strong>Kept alive</strong> — kept, but alive. Know your local rules on what you can do with a live animal. - <strong>Released</strong> — let go on the spot. - <strong>Transported</strong> — hauled out and released elsewhere.</p>\n<ol><li>Add a note if you want (\"big boar, tore up the set\"), or tap</li></ol>\n<p><strong>🎤 Record memo</strong> and talk instead of typing — the recording attaches to that catch, playable later from the set's memo list.</p>\n<ol><li>Save.</li></ol>\n<p>The app snapshots the set's bait, lure, and trap onto the log entry, so even if you change the set later, the record of <em>that catch</em> stays honest.</p>\n<p><strong>iPhone folks:</strong> there's no record button in the app on iPhone — Apple's speech engine can't be reached from the installed app, so the app doesn't pretend otherwise. Tap the notes box, hit the 🎤 key on your keyboard, and dictate. Same field, same result.</p>\n<h3>\"Other events\" — the stuff that isn't a catch</h3>\n<p>On the log screen there's a tab for other events. Use it for:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Sprung</strong> — trap fired, nothing in it. (The disposition row hides itself</li></ul>\n<p>for sprung traps — there's nothing to do with.)</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Non-target animal</strong> — caught something out of season or not a</li></ul>\n<p>furbearer; the app won't count it as a catch.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Empty — nothing changed</strong> — you checked the set and there was nothing</li></ul>\n<p>to do: no catch, no change. The sheet strips down to date and notes — one tap and the check-in is on the record (kept in your data and exports, just not shown with the catches — it files under Activity in History). Note the quick ✓ button on the set itself is different — that one only restarts the check clock and saves nothing.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Fur</strong> or <strong>Animal part</strong> — found sign worth recording.</li><li><strong>Other</strong> — anything else; describe it in the notes.</li></ul>\n<p>Logging sprung and empty traps matters — it's how your records stay honest about effort. A set that fired six times and caught twice tells a different story than one that caught twice and never fired.</p>\n<h3>Trap check reminders</h3>\n<p>Every set runs a check clock. The clock starts when the set is made and restarts every time you touch it — an edit, a catch, an event, even an Empty check-in. When an <em>active</em> set goes longer than your check interval without any activity, its pin turns red on the map so the overdue ones find you.</p>\n<ul><li>Only active sets turn red. Sprung sets keep their amber pin — that</li></ul>\n<p>already means \"come reset me.\"</p>\n<ul><li>The interval is fixed at 24 hours out of the box — that's the safe</li></ul>\n<p>default anywhere. Where the rules allow longer, change it in <strong>Settings → Trap Checks</strong> (24, 36, 48, or 72 hours). The alerts can be turned off there too — the clock keeps stamping your visits underneath, so turning it back on doesn't paint the whole map red at once.</p>\n<ul><li>Open the set to see the exact countdown: <em>Due in 6h</em> or <em>Overdue by 2d 4h</em>.</li></ul>\n<p>Right below it is a <strong>✓ Checked — nothing changed</strong> button — one tap restarts the clock and the pin goes back to green. It saves nothing to History — it's just the clock. (Want the check-in on the record? Log it as an Other → Empty event instead.)</p>\n<ul><li>Pulled sets don't get a clock — the trap's already out of the ground.</li></ul>\n<p>Missing sets don't either — that's a recovery job, not a check rhythm.</p>\n<ul><li>Old sets pick up the clock from the newest thing on record — when the</li></ul>\n<p>set was made or its latest log entry, whichever is later.</p>\n<h3>Totals — reading your season</h3>\n<p>Open the <strong>Totals</strong> tab. Everything here is for the current season, highest to lowest.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>By species:</strong> what you caught, most to least.</li><li><strong>By set:</strong> what each set produced, most to least. Each row shows the</li></ul>\n<p>title and kept count — tap it for the alive/released/transported breakdown plus the set's setup.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>By disposition:</strong> how it all shook out — dispatched, released,</li></ul>\n<p>transported, kept alive. Tap a disposition row and it breaks down by species, so you see exactly what got released and what got dispatched.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>By month:</strong> your catches grouped by calendar month, in the order the</li></ul>\n<p>season happened. The headline is the month's total — tap it for the disposition breakdown.</p>\n<ul><li>All four cards always stand. Tap a card's header to fold it shut or open</li></ul>\n<p>it back up.</p>\n<h3>Scorecard — what's actually producing</h3>\n<p>The Scorecard answers one question: what's actually putting fur up? It ranks your lures, baits, and urine by raw catch counts — not guesses, not per-night math that falls apart when you switch lures mid-season. Every catch gets stamped with what the set was carrying at that moment, so changing a set later can't rewrite your past. Sets with nothing filled in land in an \"Unspecified\" bucket at the bottom so the rankings stay honest. Filter by this season, last season, or all-time; filter by species; or pick two and run them head-to-head.</p>\n<h3>The little sponsor logos</h3>\n<p>In the bait and lure lists, sponsor rows carry the maker's logo — that's a maker who helps keep this app free. Tap the logo to read their bio: who they are, what they make, and a link to their site. Tapping it never fills anything in or picks for you; your pick is still yours. You'll also see sponsor names on the opening screen and beside your county name on the map — those are nameplates only and don't tap through to anything.</p>\n<h3>Weather lives on the map now</h3>\n<p>There's no Weather tab anymore. The weather rides on the Map as a small green chip on the left side, just above the layer button — condition icon over the temperature. Tap it and you get the full sheet: current conditions, wind, the next 12 hours, and the National Weather Service 7-day outlook with any hazard alerts. Weather still auto-attaches to every new set and catch when you've got a signal.</p>\n<h3>Wind arrows on your pins</h3>\n<p>Zoom in close (14 or tighter) and every pin grows a little wind arrow. It points where your scent is going — downwind, not where the wind's coming from. The arrows refresh on their own; turn them off in Settings under Toggles → Feature Toggles if they're clutter.</p>\n<h3>Finding a set from the Show pill</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Show</strong> pill on the map filters which sets are drawn — and the list under it names every set on the line, grouped by status (Active, Sprung, Pulled, Missing, Prospect) and A–Z inside each group. Tap a set's name and the map flies straight to it. If that set's status was filtered off, the filter switches it back on first, so you always land looking at the set you picked. The filters apply the moment you tap them — there's no Done button to hunt for.</p>\n<h3>Follow-me GPS, how it actually works</h3>\n<p>One tap on the crosshair and the map follows you — and it stays following. There's no off switch; dragging the map to look around pauses it (\"Follow paused — map dragged\") and tapping the crosshair picks it back up. The + button drops a pin at your live GPS spot right then and goes straight to the New Set form. The middle pin button is for placing somewhere you're not standing — tap the map, drag the pin onto the exact spot, hit \"Looks right.\" Pins never save on a stale location: if the GPS can't get a fresh fix, the app says \"Waiting for GPS…\" and holds the save.</p>\n<h3>Route and Mileage</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Route and Mileage</strong> section lives on the Paperwork tab. Tap <strong>Log Trip</strong> to record a run with start and end odometer readings — date, miles, notes — and the app keeps a running total for the line. It also draws a straight-line route estimate between your sets on the map (dashed line; it reads 20–40% under real road miles, so treat it as a rough check, not a receipt). Mileage exports to CSV from the Data tab.</p>\n<p>Or tap <strong>Track a route</strong> (same section, or the track button on the map) and the app records your actual path by GPS while you run the line. Stop the track and it files itself as a mileage trip, with a readout of which active sets you passed and which you missed. Short runs read in feet — under a mile, the counter shows feet instead of tenths of a mile; the trip itself still files in miles. While a track runs, the app keeps your screen awake so the recording stays unbroken. If recording is interrupted anyway — the phone sleeps, or you leave the app — the route breaks at that spot and picks back up when you return. It never draws a line across ground it didn't record, and the miles count only the path actually traveled.</p>\n<p>Trips file as one-line rows — date, miles, and whether the run was a <strong>GPS track</strong>, a <strong>Drawn route</strong>, or an <strong>Odometer</strong> entry (odometer rows show their readings right on the line). Tap a row to open it: the passed-sets readout, the odometer photos, <strong>Show on map</strong>, and the trip's editing all live inside the open row.</p>\n<p>A saved GPS route can be repaired after the fact with <strong>Fix route</strong>, inside the open trip row. Every recorded point lands on the map as a dot you can grab: drag a point to where it should be and the line reshapes around it — pull two or three points along a bend to make the line follow a curve. Tap any point once to pick it and the bar names it — and offers <strong>Remove point</strong> to take that one point out. Where two points sit on the same spot, as a closed loop's first point and its twin do, tap the spot again to reach the one underneath; the bar tells you when points are stacked. For a stretch that never happened at all — a spike to a field you never set foot in — tap the last good point before it and the first good point after it, then tap <strong>Cut</strong>: the points between come out and the route splits there honestly. <strong>Connect</strong> is the joining verb — tap any two points and it runs one straight line between them: it adds one straight line between the two points and changes nothing else — every leg already on the route stays exactly as it was, and every point stays. It works inside one stretch, across one break, and across several breaks at once, and the line always runs between the two dots you actually tapped, never through a dot you didn't. Tap the route's very first and very last points instead and the bar offers <strong>Join the ends</strong> — the route closes into a loop, with the closing stretch counted too. And every step in the editor — a connect, a cut, a drag, a drawn stretch — can be backed out with <strong>Undo</strong>, one step at a time, so a wrong move never means starting the repair over. A lone dot sitting by itself mid-route is one brief fix the app stranded between two breaks; connect it to the stretch on each side to make the route whole, or leave it be — a stranded point draws nothing and counts no miles either way. <strong>Draw</strong> covers the stretch the phone never recorded at all — no points to drag, nothing to join. Tap <strong>Draw</strong>, then tap any point to grow the route from it — or tap the map to start a stretch of its own wherever you like: each tap lays a point by hand, and the line and the miles follow. While you draw, tapping any other point aims the stretch at it — tap <strong>Connect</strong> and the drawn line runs to that point — and that includes the point you started from: tap it and the stretch closes back onto its start, whether it is the end of a stretch or a point in its middle; the route carries on from there, and nothing already drawn is rerouted. <strong>Stop here</strong> leaves the stretch open where it stands, and <strong>Undo</strong> takes back the last point laid. Points you draw are marked as drawn by hand in the record, and from then on they are ordinary points — drag them in a later session like any other. The trip's miles in the bar update as you work. Nothing is saved until you tap <strong>Done</strong>; <strong>Cancel</strong> throws the whole edit away and the trip stays exactly as it was. When you tap <strong>Save</strong>, the fixed route stays on screen so you can check it; the route bar's own <strong>Done</strong> then clears the bar and leaves you on the map, and <strong>Hide route</strong> is the button that takes the route off. While you fix or draw, the map's other tools rest — nothing else can open over your work until you are done.</p>\n<p>A route can also be drawn whole, with no track behind it at all — a line you are planning, or one you ran the day the phone stayed in the truck. Tap <strong>Draw a route</strong> beside Log Trip: the map opens empty, you tap along the route while the miles count up, <strong>Undo</strong> takes back a point, and <strong>Save</strong> asks for the date and files it as a trip on the line. A point that lands wrong is fixed right there, not after: drag it to where it belongs, or tap it and <strong>Remove this dot</strong>. Tap the first point instead and the bar offers <strong>Close the loop</strong> — the route ties back to its start, with the closing stretch counted in the miles. It reads <strong>Drawn route</strong> in the log, so the record always says how the route came to be, and its miles count in the line's total like any other trip. From there it is a trip like any other: <strong>Show on map</strong> draws it, <strong>Fix route</strong> opens on it, and it deletes the same way.</p>\n<p>The on/off switch is <strong>Mileage log</strong> in Settings → Toggles → Feature Toggles, and it only governs recording <em>new</em> trips. Turn it off and your recorded trips, the Route and Mileage section, and the CSV export all stay right where they were — you just can't add new ones until you turn it back on. The section only disappears when a line has no trips recorded at all.</p>\n<h3>Bringing in Google Maps pins</h3>\n<p>Got pins saved in Google Maps lists? You don't need to walk them in one by one. On your phone's browser, go to takeout.google.com, tap \"Deselect all,\" check only <strong>Saved</strong>, and start the export. When Google emails you that it's ready, download it, tap the file in your Files app to unzip it, and look in the <strong>Saved</strong> folder — there's one spreadsheet (.csv) file per list. Then, in Opossum Foot: switch to the trap line the pins belong on first (imports land on the line that's active), open the <strong>Data</strong> tab, look under <strong>Move data in or out</strong>, and use <strong>Import pins from Google Maps</strong> to pick that file. It also reads map files (.kml / .kmz) and place files (.geojson / .json) from Google Maps or My Maps. The app digs each pin's coordinates out of its Maps link itself, carries the names over, and names any pin that truly has no location instead of planting it in the ocean. If a pin lands on top of a set you already have, the app stops and asks — skip it, merge it, or delete the incoming pin. Your app data is never touched by an import. Just one pin? Share it from Google Maps, copy the link, and use <strong>Paste a Maps link</strong> in the same place. Either way, you fill in the trap and attractant details afterward by tapping each pin on the map.</p>\n<h3>Photos — the gallery, zoom, and sharing them</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Photo gallery</strong> button on the Paperwork tab, beside the License Wallet, gathers the active line's photos in one grid, each labeled by what it belongs to — a set, a catch, or paperwork. Tap any photo anywhere in the app to open it full screen, and pinch to zoom — zoom works on every photo, not just some.</p>\n<p>Every photo viewer has a <strong>Save Photo</strong> button. It hands the photo to your phone's share sheet — one more tap and it's in your library. Handy for the license photos and the set-build shots.</p>\n<p><strong>Sharing photos with another trapper:</strong> in the Photo gallery, tap <strong>Share photo pack</strong>. That builds one file carrying the line's set and catch photos (license photos and other paperwork stay home) and hands it to your share sheet — AirDrop it, send it, however you like. On the receiving phone, use <strong>Import data</strong> on the Data tab, under <strong>Move data in or out</strong>, and pick the pack: each photo re-attaches to the set with the same name on the imported line, and catch photos match up to their catch by date and species. If the sets aren't on that phone yet, import the line share first (see the next section), then the pack. The same pack can't be added twice.</p>\n<h3>Sharing a whole line</h3>\n<p>Handing a line to a buddy, or moving it to a new phone? On the <strong>Data</strong> tab, under <strong>Move data in or out</strong>, tap <strong>Share this line</strong>. One file carries the line's sets and catch logs — share it by AirDrop or Nearby Share. On the receiving phone, Import brings it in as a <strong>new line</strong> of its own, beside the lines already there: nothing merges, and nothing already on that phone is changed. Photos don't ride in a line share — they travel in a photo pack, above. The same file can't be imported twice.</p>\n<h3>Deleting means gone</h3>\n<p>Delete means gone, everywhere. Deleting a set takes its pin, its catch logs out of History, its photos, and its voice memos — the app warns you plainly before it happens. You can also delete a single catch from History or from the set's log list, same gone-forever warning.</p>\n<p><strong>Erase Everything</strong> (Settings) makes you prove it's really you — a quick math question and typing DELETE EVERYTHING — so a kid tapping around can't wipe your season.</p>\n<h3>Seasons — the reminders</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Seasons</strong> tab lists your state's furbearers A to Z. Tap one for its season dates, bag limits, and any notes. A badge shows whether it's open, closed, or coming up.</p>\n<p>Iowa trappers get two extras at the top of the tab: a <strong>Trap &amp; Snare Rules</strong> quick reference (the short version of the state's trap and snare rules) and a <strong>Find your game warden</strong> button that opens the DNR's county directory. Same rule as everything else here — reminders, not legal authority.</p>\n<p>Read the red line at the top of the tab: this is a **reminder, not legal authority**. Seasons change, emergency orders happen, counties differ. Check your state agency's current regulations before you trap — the app will never tell you it's legal to set a trap.</p>\n<h3>Paperwork</h3>\n<p>Open the <strong>Paperwork</strong> tab and tap \"📷 Add license photo.\" Snap your license — and your kid's or your buddy's if you carry theirs. They're just photos in a row, so snap them in an order you'll remember. It lives on your phone so it's there when the warden asks, even with no signal.</p>\n<p>The tab also holds the <strong>landowner notebook</strong>: names, phone numbers, permission slips, and boundary notes for every property you run sets on. Tap a landowner's number to call or text them straight from the notebook — no copying numbers out of the app.</p>\n<p><strong>Tag records</strong> live here too, in the <strong>My Tags</strong> section. In half the states, some pelts — bobcat and otter in Iowa — have to be tagged or sealed, and the tag number is the kind of paper that gets lost. When you log a catch of a species your state tags, the catch form offers a small <strong>Tag</strong> section: the tag or sealing number, the date you got it tagged, and a photo of the tag on the pelt. The same three fields sit under the tagging note on the \"Catch logged!\" sheet, too. Every part of it is optional, and an untouched section changes nothing — the catch saves just like always. Most tagging happens days after the catch, at a station, so you can fill it in later: open the catch in History and tap <strong>Tag record</strong>, or open My Tags here, where every tagged catch — and every catch still waiting on its tag, with the state's deadline beside it — is gathered in one place for when the warden or the fur buyer asks. If you never trap a species that gets tagged, turn <strong>Tag records (catch sheet + My Tags)</strong> off under Settings → Toggles → Feature Toggles and the whole thing disappears; anything you already saved stays saved.</p>\n<h3>Backing up — read this one twice</h3>\n<p>There is no account and no cloud. Everything lives on your phone, which means a lost phone, a wiped phone, or clearing your browser data can take your whole season with it. Open the <strong>Data</strong> tab — backups, sharing, and all the reports live in that one place.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Keep your data safe</strong> is the top card: <strong>\"Back up all data (JSON)\"</strong> — sets,</li></ul>\n<p>catches, lines, landowners, the lot. Importing it restores everything. Do this one, not just the CSVs.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Your pictures ride separately:</strong> <strong>Export photos &amp; files (ZIP)</strong> saves</li></ul>\n<p>every photo and voice memo from every line — set and catch photos, licenses, permission slips — as one zip file with an index saying what each file is. The JSON backup holds your data but not your pictures; this file is their backup. And it is not one-way: bring that same ZIP back through <strong>Import data</strong> on the Data tab and every picture and memo goes back where it belongs.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Reports</strong> is below it: set the date range and the filter dropdowns</li></ul>\n<p>(each one opens a checklist — pick what you want and tap Done), watch the match count (\"37 of 210 catches match\"), then download the report you want. The filters shape the catches report — the sets and mileage downloads always include everything.</p>\n<ul><li>Do it at least a few times a season. If the phone goes in the creek, the</li></ul>\n<p>JSON file is your season.</p>\n<ul><li>License photos and voice memos don't ride in the JSON backup — they're</li></ul>\n<p>in the photo ZIP above.</p>\n<h3>If something looks wrong</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>The app looks old or stuck after an update:</strong> force-quit your browser</li></ul>\n<p>completely (swipe it away) and reopen the app from the home-screen icon. Nine times out of ten, that fixes it.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Tabs or features went missing:</strong> they're probably just toggled off.</li></ul>\n<p>Check Settings — Tabs on the Bottom Bar and Feature Toggles are per trap line, so one line can run lean while another keeps everything.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Your location dot won't show:</strong> check that you allowed location access</li></ul>\n<p>for the browser, and that you're not in airplane mode.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Something's genuinely broken or confusing:</strong> tell Tanner. That's what the</li></ul>\n<p>beta is for — he'd rather hear about it than have you fight it.</p>\n<h3>The beta deal</h3>\n<p>This is a closed beta — a small circle on purpose. Please don't pass the link around. If something looks wrong, or you want something the app doesn't do yet, tell Tanner directly.</p>\n<h3>The fine print</h3>\n<p>Season data is a reminder reference only — not legal authority. Know your regulations, check your local ordinances, and trap legal.</p>";
